  { id: "mhc-lata-watan", type: "historical story", domain: "history", region: "mumbai", title: "Lata Mangeshkar and the song that made Nehru cry", value: 5, cue: "patriotic songs, Republic Day, or Lata Mangeshkar", story: "mhc-watan",
    emoji: "🎤", wiki: "Lata Mangeshkar",
    fact: "Lata Mangeshkar sang in Hindi films for over 70 years and received the Bharat Ratna in 2001. In January 1963, after the war with China, she sang “Ae Mere Watan Ke Logon” for the first time at a Republic Day event in Delhi. Prime Minister Nehru was moved to tears.",
    why: "The song is still played every Republic Day and Independence Day. It shows how one voice can carry the grief of a whole country.",
    mech: "India had lost the 1962 war with China, and the mood was heavy. The poet Pradeep wrote the first lines on the foil of a cigarette packet while walking on Mahim beach in Mumbai. C. Ramchandra set them to music. Lata first said no, because there was little time to practise, but Pradeep persuaded her. The song was never part of a film. Its royalties were promised to the fund for war widows.",
    models: ["availability-bias"],
    q: "What is the story behind “Ae Mere Watan Ke Logon”?",
    a: "After the 1962 war with China, Pradeep wrote the song on a cigarette packet at Mahim beach. Lata Mangeshkar sang it in Delhi in January 1963 at a Republic Day event, and Nehru wept.",
    chain: [["India had just lost the 1962 war with China", ["1962", "china", "war"]], ["Pradeep wrote the words on a cigarette packet at Mahim", ["pradeep", "cigarette", "mahim", "wrote"]], ["Lata sang it in Delhi in January 1963", ["1963", "delhi", "republic day", "sang"]], ["Nehru was moved to tears", ["nehru", "tears", "cried", "wept"]]],
    mcq: { q: "What was “Ae Mere Watan Ke Logon” written about?", o: ["Soldiers who died in the 1962 war with China", "The 1947 Partition", "The 1971 war with Pakistan", "The Quit India movement"] },
    bring: "When the song plays on 26 January: “This was never a film song. Pradeep wrote it on a cigarette packet at Mahim beach.”",
    follow: "Which song makes you feel something every single time you hear it?",
    deeper: "Lata was born in Indore in 1929 to a Marathi family from Goa. Her father died when she was 13, and she began singing to feed the family. She died in Mumbai on 6 February 2022, aged 92." },

  { id: "mhc-asha-bhosle", type: "person", domain: "history", region: "mumbai", title: "Asha Bhosle: the most recorded singer", value: 4, cue: "old film songs, Guinness records, or Asha Bhosle",
    emoji: "🎶", wiki: "Asha Bhosle",
    fact: "In 2011 Guinness World Records recognised Asha Bhosle for the most studio recordings by any singer. She had recorded up to 11,000 songs in more than 20 languages since 1947. She died in Mumbai on 12 April 2026, aged 92.",
    why: "She worked for decades in the shadow of her elder sister Lata, and then built a sound of her own. The record came late, at 78.",
    mech: "Lata Mangeshkar got most of the soft, heroine songs. Asha took the songs others did not want: cabaret, folk, ghazal, playful duets. That made her range very wide. Music directors like O. P. Nayyar and R. D. Burman wrote boldly for that voice. Over 60 years, the variety added up to a number no one else has matched.",
    models: ["circle-of-competence", "compounding"],
    q: "Why did Asha Bhosle end up with more recordings than any other singer?",
    a: "Her sister Lata had the main heroine songs, so Asha took cabaret, folk and playful songs. That wide range, sung for over 60 years, added up to about 11,000 recordings and a Guinness record in 2011.",
    chain: [["Lata held the main heroine songs", ["lata", "sister", "shadow"]], ["Asha took the songs others did not want", ["cabaret", "folk", "range", "variety", "others did not"]], ["Composers wrote boldly for her voice", ["nayyar", "burman", "composer", "music director", "wrote"]], ["About 11,000 songs and a Guinness record in 2011", ["11,000", "11000", "guinness", "2011", "record"]]],
    mcq: { q: "What did Guinness World Records recognise Asha Bhosle for in 2011?", o: ["The most studio recordings by any singer", "The longest single concert", "The highest note ever sung", "The most awards won in one year"] },
    bring: "When old film songs play: “Asha Bhosle recorded around 11,000 songs. Guinness gave her the record in 2011, when she was 78.”",
    follow: "Is it better to be the best at one thing, or good at many things nobody else wants to do?",
    deeper: "Asha was born in Sangli in 1933. She also loved cooking and ran restaurants named “Asha's” in Dubai and other cities." },

  { id: "mhc-pu-la", type: "person", domain: "language", region: "maharashtra", title: "Pu La Deshpande and Marathi humour", value: 4, cue: "Marathi books, comedy, or Pune",
    emoji: "😄", wiki: "P. L. Deshpande",
    fact: "Purushottam Laxman Deshpande, known as Pu La, was the best-loved Marathi humorist of the 20th century. His “Batatyachi Chaal” (1958) and “Vyakti ani Valli” (1966) are still read in almost every Marathi home. He was also an actor, a harmonium player and a one-man stage performer.",
    why: "He made ordinary people laugh at themselves kindly. Marathi readers still call him “Maharashtra's beloved personality”.",
    mech: "Pu La wrote about the people everyone knew: the tenant in a Mumbai chawl, the cook, the talkative neighbour. He drew their small habits with love, not mockery. He then read these pieces aloud on stage, so his voice and timing became part of the writing. He gave away much of his earnings, including large sums to Baba Amte's Anandwan.",
    models: ["social-proof"],
    q: "Why does Pu La Deshpande hold such a special place in Marathi culture?",
    a: "He wrote warm humour about ordinary people, as in Batatyachi Chaal and Vyakti ani Valli. He performed the pieces on stage himself, and he gave much of his money to causes like Anandwan.",
    chain: [["He wrote about ordinary people with warmth", ["ordinary", "chawl", "neighbour", "warm", "kind"]], ["Batatyachi Chaal and Vyakti ani Valli", ["batatyachi", "vyakti", "valli", "chaal"]], ["He performed his own writing on stage", ["stage", "performed", "read", "one-man", "voice"]], ["He gave away much of his money", ["gave", "donat", "anandwan", "charity", "earnings"]]],
    mcq: { q: "Which of these is a famous book by Pu La Deshpande?", o: ["Batatyachi Chaal", "Yayati", "Natsamrat", "Fakira"] },
    bring: "When Marathi humour comes up: “Pu La wrote about a potato chawl in Mumbai in 1958, and people still quote it today.”",
    follow: "Which writer or comedian makes you laugh at your own family without feeling hurt?",
    deeper: "Pu La was born in Mumbai in 1919 and died in Pune in 2000. “Vyakti ani Valli” won the Sahitya Akademi Award. He received the Padma Bhushan in 1990 and the first Maharashtra Bhushan award in 1996." },

  { id: "mhc-tendulkar-debut", type: "person", domain: "history", region: "mumbai", title: "Sachin Tendulkar: debut at 16, 100 hundreds", value: 4, cue: "cricket, child prodigies, or Sachin",
    emoji: "🏏", wiki: "Sachin Tendulkar",
    fact: "Sachin Tendulkar played his first Test for India on 15 November 1989 in Karachi, aged 16 years and 205 days. He went on to score 100 international centuries, a record nobody else has reached. He retired in November 2013 after his 200th Test.",
    why: "A Mumbai schoolboy became the most consistent batsman the game has seen. The lesson is less about talent and more about 24 years of daily work.",
    mech: "In 1988 Sachin and Vinod Kambli put on a 664-run partnership in a school match for Shardashram Vidyamandir. Selectors noticed. In Pakistan a Waqar Younis bouncer bloodied his nose, but he refused to leave the field. That toughness, plus a simple technique and constant practice, kept him in the team for 24 years. Long careers are how big totals are built.",
    models: ["compounding", "margin-of-safety"],
    q: "How did Sachin Tendulkar reach 100 international hundreds?",
    a: "He started at 16 in Karachi in 1989 after a famous 664-run school partnership. Toughness and a simple technique kept him playing for 24 years, and the long career built the 100 centuries.",
    chain: [["A 664-run school partnership got him noticed", ["664", "kambli", "school", "shardashram"]], ["Test debut at 16 in Karachi, 1989", ["16", "sixteen", "karachi", "1989"]], ["Toughness and simple technique kept him in the team", ["tough", "nose", "technique", "practice", "refused"]], ["A 24-year career built 100 hundreds", ["24 years", "100", "hundred", "centur", "long career"]]],
    mcq: { q: "How old was Sachin Tendulkar when he played his first Test?", o: ["16", "18", "19", "21"] },
    bring: "When someone says they are too young for something: “Sachin played for India at 16, with a bleeding nose in Pakistan.”",
    follow: "What is one skill you would still be improving at after 24 years?",
    deeper: "His 100th century came in March 2012 against Bangladesh in Dhaka. In 2014 he became the youngest person to receive the Bharat Ratna. He grew up in Bandra East and practised at Shivaji Park." },

  { id: "mhc-gavaskar-10000", type: "person", domain: "history", region: "mumbai", title: "Gavaskar: first to 10,000 Test runs", value: 3, cue: "cricket records, opening batsmen, or Gavaskar",
    emoji: "🧢", wiki: "Sunil Gavaskar",
    fact: "In March 1987, against Pakistan at Ahmedabad, Sunil Gavaskar became the first batsman in history to score 10,000 Test runs. He had already passed Don Bradman's record of 29 Test centuries in 1983. He did all this without a helmet, facing the fastest bowlers of his time.",
    why: "Before Gavaskar, Indian batsmen were seen as weak against pace. He opened the batting against the West Indies and proved that wrong.",
    mech: "An opener faces the new ball and the freshest fast bowlers. Gavaskar's method was patience and a very straight bat. In his first series in the West Indies in 1971 he scored 774 runs, still the record for a debut series. Facing the best attack in the world without a helmet forced perfect technique. Over 16 years, that technique turned into 10,000 runs.",
    models: ["margin-of-safety", "compounding"],
    q: "What made Sunil Gavaskar's 10,000 Test runs so remarkable?",
    a: "He was an opener who faced the fastest bowlers with no helmet, using patience and a straight bat. He scored 774 runs in his debut series in 1971 and became the first to 10,000 Test runs in 1987.",
    chain: [["Openers face the new ball and fresh fast bowlers", ["opener", "open", "new ball", "fast bowl", "pace"]], ["Patience and a straight bat, no helmet", ["patience", "straight", "helmet", "technique"]], ["774 runs in his first series in 1971", ["774", "1971", "debut series", "west indies"]], ["First to 10,000 Test runs in 1987", ["10,000", "10000", "ten thousand", "1987"]]],
    mcq: { q: "Who was the first batsman to score 10,000 Test runs?", o: ["Sunil Gavaskar", "Don Bradman", "Sachin Tendulkar", "Vivian Richards"] },
    bring: "When cricket records come up: “Gavaskar got to 10,000 Test runs before anyone in the world, and he never wore a helmet.”",
    follow: "Which is harder: being the first to do something, or beating a record that already exists?",
    deeper: "Gavaskar wrote in his book “Sunny Days” that he was swapped with a fisherman's baby at birth. An uncle spotted a birthmark near his ear and found the right baby." },

  { id: "mhc-hazare-first-win", type: "person", domain: "history", region: "maharashtra", title: "Vijay Hazare and India's first Test win", value: 3, cue: "cricket history, Sangli, or domestic cricket trophies",
    emoji: "🏆", wiki: "Vijay Hazare",
    fact: "Vijay Hazare, from Sangli, captained India to its first ever Test victory in February 1952, against England at Madras. In 1948 he scored centuries in both innings of a Test at Adelaide against Don Bradman's great Australian team. India's domestic one-day trophy carries his name.",
    why: "India played Tests from 1932 and did not win one for 20 years. Hazare was the captain who finally ended the wait.",
    mech: "In the early years Indian teams were new to Test cricket and often divided. Hazare was a quiet, steady batsman who led by example. His 116 and 145 at Adelaide in 1948 showed Indians could score against the best attack in the world. Four years later his team bowled England out twice at Madras and won by an innings.",
    models: ["lindy-effect"],
    q: "Why is Vijay Hazare remembered in Indian cricket?",
    a: "He scored two centuries in one Test at Adelaide in 1948 against Bradman's Australia. As captain he led India to its first Test win, against England at Madras in 1952.",
    chain: [["India waited 20 years for a first Test win", ["20 years", "twenty", "1932", "waited", "first ever"]], ["Twin centuries at Adelaide in 1948", ["adelaide", "1948", "both innings", "116", "145", "two centuries"]], ["Captain of the 1952 win at Madras", ["1952", "madras", "captain", "england"]]],
    mcq: { q: "Against whom did India win its first Test match in 1952?", o: ["England", "Australia", "West Indies", "Pakistan"] },
    bring: "When the Vijay Hazare Trophy is on TV: “Hazare was the captain when India won a Test for the first time, in 1952.”",
    follow: "Do you know which team India beat to win its first one-day international?",
    deeper: "In a 1943 match Hazare scored 309 out of a team total of 387. He was among the first cricketers to receive the Padma Shri, in 1960. He died in 2004." },

  { id: "mhc-shahu-reservation", type: "event", domain: "history", region: "maharashtra", title: "Shahu Maharaj's 1902 reservation order", value: 5, cue: "reservations, Kolhapur, or social justice",
    emoji: "📜", wiki: "Shahu of Kolhapur",
    fact: "On 26 July 1902, Chhatrapati Shahu Maharaj of Kolhapur ordered that 50% of all new posts in his state government go to people from backward communities. It is counted as the first reservation policy in modern India.",
    why: "Reservations are debated across India today. The idea began in a small princely state in Maharashtra, more than 45 years before the Constitution.",
    mech: "In Kolhapur state almost all official jobs were held by a few upper-caste groups, because only they had schooling. Shahu Maharaj saw that merit alone could not fix this, since the starting lines were unequal. So he reserved half the new posts and also opened free hostels and schools for many communities. Over time, people from these communities gained education and government experience.",
    models: ["feedback-loops", "first-principles"],
    q: "What did Shahu Maharaj do in 1902 and why?",
    a: "He reserved 50% of new government posts in Kolhapur for backward communities, because a few groups held almost all jobs. He also built hostels and schools so those communities could qualify.",
    chain: [["A few groups held almost all official jobs", ["few", "upper", "almost all", "held", "schooling"]], ["50% of new posts reserved, 26 July 1902", ["50", "half", "1902", "reserved", "reservation"]], ["Hostels and schools opened for many communities", ["hostel", "school", "education", "free"]], ["Those communities gained education and experience", ["gained", "over time", "experience", "qualify"]]],
    mcq: { q: "What did Shahu Maharaj order in 1902?", o: ["50% of new government posts for backward communities", "Free land for all farmers", "A ban on child marriage", "English as the court language"] },
    bring: "When reservations come up: “The first reservation order in India was in Kolhapur in 1902, long before Independence.”",
    follow: "What do you think should decide whether a policy like this has done its job?",
    deeper: "Shahu Maharaj also supported the young Dr B. R. Ambedkar's education and backed the Satyashodhak movement started by Jyotirao Phule. Maharashtra marks his birthday, 26 June, as Social Justice Day." },

  { id: "mhc-gokhale-guru", type: "person", domain: "history", region: "maharashtra", title: "Gokhale: Gandhi's political guru", value: 4, cue: "Gandhi, Pune, or mentors",
    emoji: "🎓", wiki: "Gopal Krishna Gokhale",
    fact: "Gopal Krishna Gokhale, a professor at Fergusson College in Pune, was the man Gandhi called his political guru. In 1905 Gokhale founded the Servants of India Society to train full-time workers for the nation. When Gandhi returned from South Africa in 1915, Gokhale told him to spend a year travelling India before speaking in public.",
    why: "Gandhi's deep knowledge of village India came from that year of listening. The advice of a mentor shaped the whole freedom movement.",
    mech: "Gokhale believed in patient, fact-based reform and worked inside the legislative council. He saw that Gandhi knew South Africa well but not India. So he made Gandhi promise to travel for a year with his ears open and his mouth shut. Gandhi crossed the country in third-class train compartments. Gokhale died in February 1915, in the middle of that year.",
    models: ["map-vs-territory", "circle-of-competence"],
    q: "What was Gokhale's advice to Gandhi, and why did it matter?",
    a: "Gokhale, a Pune professor who founded the Servants of India Society in 1905, told Gandhi in 1915 to travel India for a year before speaking. That year of listening gave Gandhi his knowledge of village India.",
    chain: [["Gokhale believed in patient, fact-based reform", ["patient", "fact", "reform", "council", "moderate"]], ["Founded the Servants of India Society in 1905", ["servants of india", "1905", "society"]], ["Told Gandhi to travel India for a year", ["year", "travel", "listen", "ears", "mouth shut"]], ["Gandhi came to know village India", ["village", "third-class", "knowledge", "know"]]],
    mcq: { q: "What did Gokhale ask Gandhi to do when he returned to India in 1915?", o: ["Travel the country for a year before speaking in public", "Start a newspaper in Bombay", "Join the Indian Civil Service", "Return to South Africa"] },
    bring: "When someone starts a new job: “Gokhale told Gandhi to spend his first year in India only listening. Not a bad rule.”",
    follow: "Who gave you advice that changed what you did for years afterwards?",
    deeper: "Gokhale was born in 1866 in Ratnagiri district. He and Tilak were both from Pune but disagreed sharply: Tilak wanted political freedom first, Gokhale wanted social reform first." },

  { id: "mhc-vinoba-bhoodan", type: "person", domain: "history", region: "maharashtra", title: "Vinoba Bhave and the gift of land", value: 4, cue: "land reform, charity, or Gandhi's followers",
    emoji: "🚶", wiki: "Vinoba Bhave",
    fact: "Vinoba Bhave, born in 1895 in Raigad district, started the Bhoodan movement on 18 April 1951 in the village of Pochampally in Telangana. He asked landowners to give land as a gift to the landless. Over about 13 years of walking across India, he collected pledges for more than 40 lakh acres.",
    why: "He tried to solve the land problem with no law and no force, only by asking. No other land movement anywhere has worked this way on this scale.",
    mech: "In Pochampally, landless families told Vinoba they needed 80 acres. A landlord named Ram Chandra Reddy stood up and offered 100 acres. Vinoba saw a method in this. He walked from village to village, asking every landowner to treat him as a sixth son and give one sixth of the land. Many gave. But much of the land was poor or disputed, and only about a third was actually handed over to the landless.",
    models: ["reciprocity", "social-proof"],
    q: "What was Bhoodan, and how well did it work?",
    a: "In 1951 at Pochampally a landlord offered 100 acres when Vinoba asked. Vinoba then walked across India for years asking landowners to gift land, and collected pledges for over 40 lakh acres, but only about a third reached the landless.",
    chain: [["Started at Pochampally in 1951", ["pochampally", "1951", "telangana"]], ["A landlord offered 100 acres when asked", ["100 acres", "reddy", "landlord", "offered"]], ["Vinoba walked across India asking for land", ["walk", "village to village", "asking", "sixth"]], ["Over 40 lakh acres pledged, about a third distributed", ["40 lakh", "4 million", "million acres", "third", "pledge"]]],
    mcq: { q: "How did Vinoba Bhave collect land for the landless?", o: ["By walking village to village and asking owners to gift it", "By passing a law in Parliament", "By buying it with donated money", "By occupying unused land"] },
    bring: "When charity comes up: “Vinoba Bhave walked across India for 13 years asking people to gift land. He got pledges for over 40 lakh acres.”",
    follow: "Would you give one sixth of what you own if a stranger asked you with no pressure at all?",
    deeper: "Gandhi chose Vinoba as the first individual satyagrahi in 1940. He was the first person to receive the Ramon Magsaysay Award, in 1958, and got the Bharat Ratna in 1983 after his death." },

  { id: "mhc-baba-amte", type: "historical story", domain: "history", region: "maharashtra", title: "Baba Amte and Anandwan", value: 5, cue: "leprosy, social work, or Chandrapur", story: "mhc-anandwan",
    emoji: "🌳", wiki: "Baba Amte",
    fact: "Baba Amte, a wealthy lawyer from Warora in Chandrapur district, gave up his comfortable life to serve people with leprosy. On 15 August 1949 he and his wife Sadhana started Anandwan, the Forest of Joy. It grew into a self-supporting village where patients farm, make goods and run workshops.",
    why: "He turned the most feared disease in India into a community that produced instead of begged. Dignity, not pity, was the cure he offered.",
    mech: "One night Amte saw a man with advanced leprosy lying in the rain, and he ran away in fear. He was ashamed of that fear and decided to face it. He later let himself be injected with bacteria from a patient to show the disease did not spread easily. At Anandwan, patients were given work, not charity. Working together, they built fields, schools and workshops that paid for themselves.",
    models: ["inversion", "incentives"],
    q: "What did Baba Amte do, and what was his method?",
    a: "He left a comfortable lawyer's life after a leprosy patient in the rain made him face his fear. In 1949 he started Anandwan, where patients worked and built a self-supporting village instead of begging.",
    chain: [["A leprosy patient in the rain made him face his fear", ["rain", "fear", "ashamed", "patient"]], ["He left a comfortable life as a lawyer", ["lawyer", "wealthy", "comfortable", "gave up"]], ["Started Anandwan in 1949", ["anandwan", "1949", "forest"]], ["Work, not charity, built a self-supporting village", ["work", "self", "charity", "dignity", "farm"]]],
    mcq: { q: "What was Anandwan?", o: ["A self-supporting village for people with leprosy", "A tiger reserve near Nagpur", "A school for tribal children", "A hospital for the blind"] },
    bring: "When social work comes up: “Baba Amte started Anandwan in 1949 with leprosy patients. They built a village that paid for itself.”",
    follow: "What fear of your own would you most like to face and get past?",
    deeper: "He received the Ramon Magsaysay Award in 1985 and the Padma Vibhushan in 1986. In the 1990s he lived for years on the banks of the Narmada in protest against large dams. He died in 2008, aged 93." },

  { id: "mhc-hemalkasa", type: "person", domain: "history", region: "maharashtra", title: "Prakash and Mandakini Amte at Hemalkasa", value: 4, cue: "tribal areas, Gadchiroli, or doctors who work in villages",
    emoji: "🩺", wiki: "Prakash Amte",
    fact: "In December 1973, Baba Amte's son Dr Prakash Amte and his wife Dr Mandakini moved to Hemalkasa, deep in the forests of Gadchiroli. They started the Lok Biradari Prakalp to serve the Madia Gond tribal people. In 2008 they received the Ramon Magsaysay Award.",
    why: "Two young doctors chose a place with no road, no electricity and no shared language over a city career. They are still there, 50 years later.",
    mech: "The Madia Gond people had almost no contact with doctors and did not speak Marathi. The Amtes began by treating patients under a tree and learned the language slowly. Trust grew when people saw sick relatives recover. A hospital, a residential school and a home for orphaned wild animals followed. Children from that school became doctors, teachers and officers.",
    models: ["lindy-effect", "feedback-loops"],
    q: "What did Prakash and Mandakini Amte build at Hemalkasa?",
    a: "From 1973 they treated the Madia Gond tribal people in the Gadchiroli forest, first under a tree, and slowly won trust. A hospital, a school and an animal home grew from it, and they got the Magsaysay Award in 2008.",
    chain: [["They moved to the Gadchiroli forest in 1973", ["1973", "gadchiroli", "forest", "hemalkasa"]], ["Treated the Madia Gond people, first under a tree", ["madia", "gond", "tribal", "tree", "treated"]], ["Trust grew as patients recovered", ["trust", "recover", "slowly", "language"]], ["A hospital, a school and an animal home followed", ["hospital", "school", "animal", "orphan"]]],
    mcq: { q: "Whom did the Amtes serve at Hemalkasa?", o: ["The Madia Gond tribal people", "Leprosy patients from Mumbai", "Cotton farmers of Vidarbha", "Fishing families of the Konkan"] },
    bring: "When someone says one person cannot change much: “Two doctors went to a forest in Gadchiroli in 1973. There is a hospital and a school there now.”",
    follow: "What would you need to give up to work in a place like that for even one year?",
    deeper: "Prakash Amte gave up a surgery degree to go. Their animal home began with one orphaned monkey and later housed leopards, hyenas and bears whose parents had been killed." },

  { id: "mhc-bang-newborn", type: "scientific principle", domain: "science", region: "maharashtra", title: "The Bangs and home-based newborn care", value: 5, cue: "child health, village doctors, or how research changes policy",
    emoji: "👶", wiki: "Abhay Bang",
    fact: "Dr Abhay Bang and Dr Rani Bang run SEARCH in Gadchiroli. In a trial from 1993 to 1998, they trained village women to visit newborns at home and treat infections. Newborn deaths in those villages fell by 62%. The study was published in The Lancet in 1999.",
    why: "Most newborn deaths in India happened at home, far from hospitals. The Bangs showed the fix could also happen at home, for about 5 dollars per baby.",
    mech: "Babies in remote villages died of infection, cold and low birth weight before any doctor saw them. The Bangs picked 39 villages and trained a local woman in each to visit every newborn, keep it warm, help with feeding and give antibiotics for sepsis. Deaths were compared with 47 villages that got no such care. The results were so clear that India made Home-Based Newborn Care a national programme in 2011, and the WHO adopted the idea.",
    models: ["first-principles", "bayesian-thinking"],
    q: "What did the Bangs' Gadchiroli trial show?",
    a: "They trained village women to visit newborns at home and treat infections. From 1993 to 1998 newborn deaths fell by 62% compared with control villages, and India adopted the model as a national programme.",
    chain: [["Newborns died at home before any doctor saw them", ["died at home", "remote", "infection", "before"]], ["Village women trained to visit and treat newborns", ["village women", "trained", "visit", "home"]], ["Deaths fell by 62% versus control villages", ["62", "fell", "control", "compared"]], ["India made it a national programme", ["national", "programme", "program", "2011", "who"]]],
    mcq: { q: "By how much did newborn deaths fall in the Bangs' trial villages?", o: ["About 62%", "About 10%", "About 25%", "They did not change"] },
    bring: "When health care in villages comes up: “In Gadchiroli, trained village women cut newborn deaths by 62%. The Lancet published it in 1999.”",
    follow: "What other problems could be solved by training the people who are already there?",
    deeper: "The couple studied public health at Johns Hopkins in the United States and chose to return to Gadchiroli, one of Maharashtra's poorest districts. They received the Padma Shri in 2018." },

  { id: "mhc-ralegan-siddhi", type: "concept", domain: "economics", region: "maharashtra", title: "Ralegan Siddhi: a village that caught its rain", value: 4, cue: "water shortages, model villages, or rural development",
    emoji: "💧", wiki: "Ralegan Siddhi",
    fact: "Ralegan Siddhi, a village in Ahmednagar district, was dry, poor and known for illegal liquor in the early 1970s. From 1975 the villagers, led by Anna Hazare, built small dams and bunds to hold rain water in the soil. Within years its wells filled, farming became possible all year, and the village became a model for all of India.",
    why: "The rain had always been the same. What changed was that the village stopped letting it run away.",
    mech: "Rain on bare, sloping land runs off fast and takes the topsoil with it. The villagers gave free labour to repair an old embankment, dig a percolation tank and build check dams along the streams. Water now soaked into the ground and refilled the wells below. The village also agreed on strict rules: no alcohol, no cutting trees, no free grazing. Fewer animals and more trees meant the soil held even more water.",
    models: ["feedback-loops", "margin-of-safety"],
    q: "How did Ralegan Siddhi change from a dry village into a model village?",
    a: "From 1975 villagers built check dams, bunds and a percolation tank so rain soaked in instead of running off. Wells filled, and rules against alcohol, tree cutting and free grazing protected the soil.",
    chain: [["Rain ran off bare land and took the soil", ["ran off", "runs off", "bare", "topsoil", "dry"]], ["Villagers built dams, bunds and a percolation tank", ["dam", "bund", "percolation", "tank", "embankment"]], ["Water soaked in and refilled the wells", ["soak", "wells", "refill", "ground"]], ["Rules on alcohol, trees and grazing protected the soil", ["alcohol", "liquor", "trees", "grazing", "rules"]]],
    mcq: { q: "What was the main change that made Ralegan Siddhi green?", o: ["Small dams and bunds that held rain water in the ground", "A canal from the Godavari", "Heavy new rainfall", "A large government factory"] },
    bring: "When drought comes up: “Ralegan Siddhi gets the same rain it always did. It just stopped letting the water run away.”",
    follow: "Where does the rain that falls on your building go?",
    deeper: "Anna Hazare received the Padma Bhushan in 1992 for this work. The village is about 87 km from Pune and became the model for Maharashtra's Adarsh Gaon, or ideal village, programme." },

  { id: "mhc-hiware-bazar", type: "historical story", domain: "economics", region: "maharashtra", title: "Hiware Bazar: a water budget for a village", value: 5, cue: "water, village economies, or turnarounds", story: "mhc-hiware",
    emoji: "🌾", wiki: "Hiware Bazar",
    fact: "Hiware Bazar in Ahmednagar district gets only about 400 mm of rain a year. In 1989 barely 12% of its land could be farmed and families were leaving. The villagers elected Popatrao Pawar as sarpanch, built watershed works and set a yearly water budget. By the 2010s, press reports counted dozens of farming families earning more than 10 lakh rupees a year.",
    why: "The village treats water like money: it counts what the rain brings in before deciding what to spend. Few cities do that.",
    mech: "Pawar was the village's only postgraduate. He first closed the liquor shops and banned tobacco. Then the village built 52 earthen bunds, 32 stone bunds, 9 check dams and 2 percolation tanks with free labour. Each year, after the monsoon, the village measures how much water the ground holds and decides which crops to grow. Water-hungry crops like sugarcane were avoided, and bore wells for irrigation were banned. Families who had left came back.",
    models: ["margin-of-safety", "second-order-thinking", "incentives"],
    q: "How did Hiware Bazar become rich with only 400 mm of rain?",
    a: "In 1989 the village elected Popatrao Pawar, closed the liquor shops and built bunds and check dams to hold rain. Every year it measures the water it has and plans crops to fit, avoiding sugarcane and irrigation bore wells.",
    chain: [["Only 400 mm of rain and 12% farmable land in 1989", ["400", "12%", "1989", "leaving", "dry"]], ["Popatrao Pawar elected; liquor shops closed", ["pawar", "sarpanch", "liquor", "elected"]], ["Bunds and check dams built with free labour", ["bund", "check dam", "percolation", "labour", "built"]], ["A yearly water budget decides the crops", ["water budget", "measure", "crops", "sugarcane", "bore"]]],
    mcq: { q: "What does Hiware Bazar do every year after the monsoon?", o: ["Measures its ground water and plans crops to fit it", "Sells its extra water to Pune", "Holds a lottery for farm land", "Drills new bore wells"] },
    bring: "When water cuts come up: “Hiware Bazar makes a water budget every year, like a household budget. The village went from leaving to rich.”",
    follow: "If Mumbai had to make a water budget, what would it cut first?",
    deeper: "Per-person income in the village rose from about 830 rupees a month in 1995 to around 30,000 rupees by the 2010s, according to Down to Earth. Pawar later headed Maharashtra's Adarsh Gaon programme." },

  { id: "mhc-annabhau-sathe", type: "person", domain: "language", region: "mumbai", title: "Annabhau Sathe: the people's poet", value: 4, cue: "Marathi literature, chawls, or writers who had no schooling",
    emoji: "✍️", wiki: "Annabhau Sathe",
    fact: "Annabhau Sathe was born in 1920 in Sangli district to a Matang family and studied only up to class four. He walked to Mumbai with his family in 1931 during a drought. He went on to write 35 novels, including “Fakira” (1959), and ballads that were sung in the mill areas of Mumbai.",
    why: "Marathi literature had belonged to the educated. Sathe brought the mill worker, the chawl and the Dalit village into it, in their own voice.",
    mech: "In Mumbai he did odd jobs and lived among mill workers. He learned to read and write as an adult, and he wrote in the language he heard around him. His powadas and lavanis were performed in the streets, so people who could not read still knew his work. Later he wrote novels about village life and caste, and “Fakira” won a state award in 1961. In 1958 he opened the first Dalit Sahitya Sammelan in Mumbai.",
    models: ["map-vs-territory"],
    q: "Who was Annabhau Sathe and what did he bring to Marathi writing?",
    a: "A Matang boy with four years of schooling who walked to Mumbai in 1931 and lived among mill workers. He wrote 35 novels like Fakira and street ballads, bringing workers and Dalit villages into Marathi literature.",
    chain: [["Class four schooling, walked to Mumbai in 1931", ["class four", "four years", "1931", "walked", "drought"]], ["Lived and worked among mill workers", ["mill", "chawl", "odd jobs", "workers"]], ["Ballads performed in the streets reached people who could not read", ["ballad", "powada", "lavani", "street", "sung"]], ["35 novels including Fakira", ["35", "fakira", "novel"]]],
    mcq: { q: "Which novel is Annabhau Sathe best known for?", o: ["Fakira", "Yayati", "Batatyachi Chaal", "Golpitha"] },
    bring: "When someone says you need degrees to write: “Annabhau Sathe studied till class four and wrote 35 novels.”",
    follow: "Whose life around us never gets written about?",
    deeper: "At the 1958 meeting he said the earth rests not on the hood of a serpent but on the hands of Dalits and workers. A statue of him was unveiled in Moscow in 2022 at a Russian library for foreign literature." },

  { id: "mhc-dhasal-golpitha", type: "person", domain: "language", region: "mumbai", title: "Namdeo Dhasal and the Dalit Panthers", value: 3, cue: "Marathi poetry, Kamathipura, or the 1970s in Mumbai",
    emoji: "📖", wiki: "Namdeo Dhasal",
    fact: "In 1972 the poet Namdeo Dhasal published “Golpitha”, poems from the red-light streets of Kamathipura where he grew up. The same year he, J. V. Pawar and others founded the Dalit Panthers in Mumbai, taking the name from the Black Panthers in America. Dhasal received the Padma Shri in 1999.",
    why: "Golpitha used words that had never been allowed into Marathi poetry. It forced readers to see a part of Mumbai they walked past.",
    mech: "Dhasal drove a taxi and lived in Kamathipura. He wrote about its streets in raw, direct Marathi, with no polishing. Older readers were shocked, but the writer Vijay Tendulkar wrote the introduction and defended the book. The Dalit Panthers, formed on 9 July 1972, protested against violence on Dalits and spread the idea of Dr Ambedkar through young people. The group split within a few years and was dissolved in 1988.",
    models: ["availability-bias"],
    q: "Why is Namdeo Dhasal's Golpitha important?",
    a: "Published in 1972, it brought the raw language of Kamathipura into Marathi poetry for the first time. The same year Dhasal co-founded the Dalit Panthers in Mumbai, and he later received the Padma Shri in 1999.",
    chain: [["Poems from Kamathipura in raw, direct Marathi", ["kamathipura", "red-light", "raw", "direct", "street"]], ["Published in 1972, shocked older readers", ["1972", "shock", "never", "first time"]], ["Co-founded the Dalit Panthers the same year", ["panther", "pawar", "founded", "same year"]], ["Padma Shri in 1999", ["padma", "1999", "sahitya"]]],
    mcq: { q: "What is “Golpitha”?", o: ["A 1972 collection of poems about Kamathipura", "A play about a Peshwa officer", "A novel about a Dalit village", "A travel book about Russia"] },
    bring: "When Mumbai's poetry comes up: “Namdeo Dhasal wrote Golpitha in 1972 about Kamathipura. Nobody had written Marathi like that before.”",
    follow: "Which part of this city do you think still has no writer?",
    deeper: "The Sahitya Akademi gave Dhasal a lifetime achievement award in 2004. He died in Mumbai in 2014." },

  { id: "mhc-shantaram-prison", type: "person", domain: "history", region: "maharashtra", title: "V. Shantaram and “Do Aankhen Barah Haath”", value: 4, cue: "old Hindi films, prisons, or Kolhapur",
    emoji: "🎬", wiki: "V. Shantaram",
    fact: "V. Shantaram, from Kolhapur, co-founded the Prabhat Film Company in 1929 and later Rajkamal Kalamandir in Mumbai in 1942. His film “Do Aankhen Barah Haath” (1957) won a Silver Bear at Berlin and a Golden Globe. It was based on a real open prison experiment in Maharashtra.",
    why: "The film argued that trust can reform a criminal better than a locked cell. It made that argument to the whole world in 1957.",
    mech: "The princely state of Aundh, near Satara, ran an open prison called Swatantrapur where prisoners farmed without walls. The writer G. D. Madgulkar told Shantaram the story. In the film a jailer takes six murderers to a ruined farm and reforms them through work and kindness. Shantaram directed and acted, and lost sight in one eye for a time after a fight scene with a bull. The film also won India's first National Award for Best Feature Film.",
    models: ["incentives", "reciprocity"],
    q: "What is “Do Aankhen Barah Haath” about, and where did the idea come from?",
    a: "A jailer takes six murderers to a farm and reforms them through work and trust. It came from the real open prison at Swatantrapur in Aundh state, and in 1957 the film won a Silver Bear at Berlin and a Golden Globe.",
    chain: [["A real open prison at Swatantrapur in Aundh", ["swatantrapur", "aundh", "open prison", "real"]], ["A jailer reforms six murderers through farm work", ["jailer", "six", "murderer", "farm", "reform"]], ["Won a Silver Bear and a Golden Globe in 1957", ["silver bear", "berlin", "golden globe", "1957"]]],
    mcq: { q: "What was “Do Aankhen Barah Haath” based on?", o: ["A real open prison in the princely state of Aundh", "A Marathi novel by Khandekar", "The life of a Kolhapur wrestler", "A British prison in the Andamans"] },
    bring: "When prison reform comes up: “A Maharashtra prison with no walls inspired a 1957 film that won at Berlin.”",
    follow: "Do you think people behave better when trusted, or when watched?",
    deeper: "Shantaram directed about 50 films over 60 years. He received the Dadasaheb Phalke Award in 1985 and died in 1990." },

  { id: "mhc-bal-gandharva", type: "person", domain: "anthropology", region: "maharashtra", title: "Bal Gandharva: the man who taught women fashion", value: 4, cue: "Marathi theatre, saris, or old Pune",
    emoji: "🎭", wiki: "Bal Gandharva",
    fact: "Narayan Shripad Rajhans, known as Bal Gandharva, was the greatest star of Marathi musical theatre, or Sangeet Natak. Women did not act on stage in his time, so he played the heroines. His sari drapes and hairstyles were so admired that women across Maharashtra copied them.",
    why: "For about forty years, a man set the fashion for Marathi women. It shows how theatre once did the job of cinema and television together.",
    mech: "Sangeet Natak mixed classical singing with drama, and audiences came mainly for the songs. Rajhans had a sweet voice and great grace, and he founded his own company, the Gandharva Natak Mandali, in 1913. His shows ran for hours and were the biggest entertainment of their day. Since he was the most seen woman on stage, his look became the style to follow. Lokmanya Tilak is said to have given him the name Bal Gandharva, the young celestial singer.",
    models: ["social-proof", "authority-bias"],
    q: "Who was Bal Gandharva and why did women copy him?",
    a: "He was the star of Marathi musical theatre who played heroines, since women did not act. His company from 1913 was the biggest entertainment of the day, so his sari drapes and hairstyles became the fashion.",
    chain: [["Women did not act, so men played heroines", ["women did not", "heroine", "female role", "played"]], ["His singing and grace made him the top star", ["voice", "singing", "grace", "star", "sangeet"]], ["Founded the Gandharva Natak Mandali in 1913", ["1913", "mandali", "company", "founded"]], ["Women copied his saris and hairstyles", ["copied", "fashion", "sari", "hairstyle", "style"]]],
    mcq: { q: "Why did Bal Gandharva play female roles?", o: ["Women did not act on stage in his time", "He lost a bet with Tilak", "The plays were written only for men", "He could not sing male parts"] },
    bring: "When fashion trends come up: “A hundred years ago Marathi women copied their sari style from a man, Bal Gandharva.”",
    follow: "Who sets fashion today, and why do we follow them?",
    deeper: "The first Marathi Sangeet Natak was Annasaheb Kirloskar's “Sangeet Shakuntal” in 1880. Bal Gandharva was born in 1888, retired in 1955 and died in 1967." },

  { id: "mhc-keshavsut-tutari", type: "person", domain: "language", region: "maharashtra", title: "Keshavsut's trumpet call for new poetry", value: 3, cue: "Marathi poetry, Konkan, or the Tutari Express",
    emoji: "🎺", wiki: "Keshavasuta",
    fact: "Krishnaji Keshav Damle, who wrote as Keshavsut, is called the father of modern Marathi poetry. Born in 1866 in Malgund near Ratnagiri, he wrote only about 135 poems and died in 1905. His poem “Tutari” (1893) is a trumpet call to sweep away the old and build the new.",
    why: "Before him, Marathi poetry was mostly about gods and kings. Keshavsut wrote about the individual, about social change and about nature, and others followed.",
    mech: "Keshavsut was a school teacher in small towns and read English Romantic poets. He used their ideas in Marathi: personal feeling, equality, protest against old customs. “Tutari” means a trumpet, and the poem asks for one loud enough to shake the sleeping. Few read him while he lived. His poems were collected and printed only in 1916, after his death, and then a whole generation took him as their model.",
    models: ["lindy-effect"],
    q: "Why is Keshavsut called the father of modern Marathi poetry?",
    a: "A small-town teacher who read English Romantic poets, he brought personal feeling and social protest into Marathi verse. His 1893 poem Tutari is a trumpet call for the new, and his 135 poems were printed only after his death in 1905.",
    chain: [["A teacher who read English Romantic poets", ["teacher", "romantic", "english", "read"]], ["Brought personal feeling and social protest into Marathi", ["personal", "feeling", "protest", "social", "equality", "individual"]], ["Tutari in 1893 is a trumpet call for the new", ["tutari", "1893", "trumpet", "new"]], ["Only 135 poems, printed after his death", ["135", "after his death", "1916", "posthum"]]],
    mcq: { q: "What does the title of Keshavsut's poem “Tutari” mean?", o: ["A trumpet", "A monsoon cloud", "A village well", "A temple bell"] },
    bring: "On the Konkan train: “The Tutari Express is named after a 1893 Marathi poem. Keshavsut was from Malgund, near Ratnagiri.”",
    follow: "Which poem or song do you think changed how people after it wrote?",
    deeper: "His house in Malgund is now a memorial. A line from “Tutari” is heard in the film “Harishchandrachi Factory” (2009), about Dadasaheb Phalke." },

  { id: "mhc-kusumagraj-day", type: "person", domain: "language", region: "maharashtra", title: "Kusumagraj and Marathi Language Day", value: 4, cue: "27 February, Marathi pride, or Nashik",
    emoji: "📅", wiki: "Kusumagraj",
    fact: "Marathi Language Day is celebrated on 27 February, the birthday of the poet Kusumagraj, born Vishnu Vaman Shirwadkar in 1912. He won the Jnanpith Award in 1987. His play “Natsamrat” (1970), about an old actor who gives everything to his children, is a landmark of Marathi theatre.",
    why: "The state chose a poet's birthday, not a battle or a border, to celebrate its language. That says something about what Maharashtra values.",
    mech: "Kusumagraj lived most of his life in Nashik and wrote poetry, plays and stories for over 60 years. His poems spoke of freedom, nature and the dignity of ordinary people. Natsamrat gave Marathi theatre its most famous role, a King Lear of the Marathi stage. After his death in 1999, the Maharashtra government marked his birthday as Marathi Bhasha Gaurav Din.",
    models: ["lindy-effect"],
    q: "Why is 27 February Marathi Language Day?",
    a: "It is the birthday of the poet Kusumagraj, born in 1912 in Nashik, who wrote Natsamrat and won the Jnanpith in 1987. After he died in 1999, Maharashtra chose the day to honour the language.",
    chain: [["Kusumagraj was born on 27 February 1912", ["27 february", "1912", "birthday", "born"]], ["Wrote Natsamrat and won the Jnanpith in 1987", ["natsamrat", "jnanpith", "1987"]], ["Lived and wrote in Nashik for 60 years", ["nashik", "60 years", "poetry", "plays"]], ["After his death the state made his birthday Marathi Language Day", ["1999", "death", "language day", "bhasha", "gaurav"]]],
    mcq: { q: "Whose birthday is Marathi Language Day?", o: ["Kusumagraj", "Pu La Deshpande", "Sant Dnyaneshwar", "Lokmanya Tilak"] },
    bring: "On 27 February: “Today is Marathi Language Day. It is Kusumagraj's birthday, the poet who wrote Natsamrat.”",
    follow: "If your mother tongue had a day, whose birthday should it be?",
    deeper: "The first Marathi writer to win the Jnanpith was V. S. Khandekar in 1974, for his novel “Yayati”. Natsamrat was made into a film in 2016 with Nana Patekar." },

  { id: "mhc-bhimsen-sawai", type: "person", domain: "anthropology", region: "maharashtra", title: "Bhimsen Joshi and the Sawai Gandharva festival", value: 4, cue: "classical music, Pune in December, or Bharat Ratna",
    emoji: "🎵", wiki: "Bhimsen Joshi",
    fact: "Pandit Bhimsen Joshi, the great voice of the Kirana gharana, received the Bharat Ratna in 2008. In 1953 he started a small concert in Pune in memory of his guru, Sawai Gandharva, who had died a year earlier. It grew into the Sawai Gandharva festival, the biggest Hindustani classical music event in India, held every December.",
    why: "A student's thank-you to his teacher became a yearly gathering of tens of thousands. It kept classical music alive as a public, not just a palace, art.",
    mech: "As a boy, Joshi ran away from home in Karnataka at 11 to find a teacher. He found Sawai Gandharva in Kundgol and learned in the old way, by serving and listening for years. A gharana is a school of singing passed from guru to student. The festival put the best singers of every gharana on one stage for ordinary ticket buyers. Joshi ran it himself until 2002.",
    models: ["network-effects", "lindy-effect"],
    q: "How did the Sawai Gandharva festival begin?",
    a: "Bhimsen Joshi, who had run away at 11 to find a guru, started a concert in Pune in 1953 in memory of his teacher Sawai Gandharva. Open to ordinary ticket buyers, it grew into India's biggest classical music festival.",
    chain: [["Joshi ran away at 11 to find a guru", ["ran away", "11", "eleven", "guru", "teacher"]], ["Learned the Kirana style from Sawai Gandharva", ["kirana", "gharana", "sawai gandharva", "kundgol"]], ["Started a memorial concert in Pune in 1953", ["1953", "pune", "memory", "concert", "started"]], ["Grew into India's biggest classical festival", ["biggest", "largest", "festival", "december", "thousand"]]],
    mcq: { q: "Why did Bhimsen Joshi start the Sawai Gandharva festival?", o: ["In memory of his guru, who had died in 1952", "To raise money for a hospital", "To celebrate the Bharat Ratna", "Because the Pune mayor asked him"] },
    bring: "When Pune in December comes up: “The Sawai Gandharva festival began in 1953 as one student's tribute to his teacher.”",
    follow: "How do you thank a teacher who changed your life?",
    deeper: "Joshi was born in Gadag, Karnataka, in 1922 and lived in Pune for most of his life. He opened the 1988 unity song “Mile Sur Mera Tumhara”. He died in 2011." },

  { id: "mhc-lavani", type: "concept", domain: "anthropology", region: "maharashtra", title: "Lavani: the dholki and the nine-yard sari", value: 4, cue: "Marathi dance, folk music, or the nauvari sari",
    emoji: "💃", wiki: "Lavani",
    fact: "Lavani is Maharashtra's best-known folk song and dance. The word comes from “lavanya”, meaning beauty. A dancer in a nine-yard nauvari sari performs to the fast beat of the dholki drum. It flourished in the 18th century under the Peshwas, when it entertained tired soldiers.",
    why: "Lavani is bold and witty, and it was performed by women at a time when women did not appear on the stage of city theatres.",
    mech: "The nauvari sari is tied like a dhoti between the legs, so the dancer can move freely. The dholki sets a quick rhythm and the dancer answers it with sharp movements of the hips and eyes. The songs are of two kinds: Nirguni, which is spiritual, and Shringari, which is about love. Lavani usually came as part of Tamasha, the travelling folk theatre of Maharashtra, performed in open village grounds.",
    models: [],
    q: "What is Lavani and how is it performed?",
    a: "A folk song and dance of Maharashtra from the Peshwa era, performed in a nine-yard nauvari sari to the dholki drum. It has spiritual Nirguni and romantic Shringari forms and is usually part of Tamasha folk theatre.",
    chain: [["Flourished under the Peshwas in the 18th century", ["peshwa", "18th", "soldier"]], ["Nine-yard nauvari sari and the dholki", ["nauvari", "nine-yard", "nine yard", "dholki", "drum"]], ["Nirguni and Shringari types", ["nirguni", "shringari", "spiritual", "love"]], ["Part of Tamasha folk theatre", ["tamasha", "folk theatre", "travelling", "village"]]],
    mcq: { q: "Which drum sets the rhythm of Lavani?", o: ["The dholki", "The tabla", "The mridangam", "The dhol of Punjab"] },
    bring: "When a Lavani plays at a wedding: “The word comes from lavanya, beauty. It was performed for Peshwa soldiers 250 years ago.”",
    follow: "Why do you think folk dances survive when so many other old things disappear?",
    deeper: "Great Lavani artists include Yamunabai Waikar and Vithabai Narayangaonkar. In Tamasha, Lavani sits alongside the Gan, a prayer to Ganesh, and the Gaulan, a comic scene with Krishna and the milkmaids." },

  { id: "mhc-powada", type: "concept", domain: "anthropology", region: "maharashtra", title: "Powada: history sung as a ballad", value: 3, cue: "Shivaji Maharaj's story, folk singers, or how history was remembered",
    emoji: "🥁", wiki: "Powada",
    fact: "A powada is a Marathi ballad that tells of a hero or a great event in a loud, fast rhythm. The oldest surviving one tells of Shivaji Maharaj's killing of Afzal Khan in 1659, composed by the poet Agnidas. The singers are called shahirs.",
    why: "For centuries most people could not read. Powadas were how ordinary Marathi people learned and remembered their own history.",
    mech: "A shahir sings the main lines and a chorus repeats them, so a crowd can join in. Simple drums keep the pace and the story builds up to the hero's great deed. Because the songs were short and rhythmic, they were easy to remember and pass on. In the 1940s and 1950s shahirs like Annabhau Sathe and Amar Shaikh used powadas for the freedom movement and the demand for a Marathi state.",
    models: ["network-effects"],
    q: "What is a powada and why did it matter?",
    a: "A Marathi ballad sung by a shahir with a chorus, the oldest about Shivaji Maharaj and Afzal Khan in 1659. It taught history to people who could not read, and later shahirs used it for the freedom movement.",
    chain: [["A ballad of a hero sung by a shahir", ["ballad", "shahir", "hero", "sung"]], ["Oldest surviving one is about Afzal Khan, 1659", ["afzal", "1659", "agnidas", "oldest"]], ["A chorus and rhythm made it easy to remember", ["chorus", "rhythm", "remember", "repeat", "drum"]], ["Used in the 20th century for political movements", ["freedom", "movement", "marathi state", "sathe", "shaikh"]]],
    mcq: { q: "What is the oldest surviving powada about?", o: ["Shivaji Maharaj and Afzal Khan", "The Battle of Panipat", "The Peshwa Bajirao", "The Pandharpur Wari"] },
    bring: "When history lessons come up: “Marathi villagers learned their history from powadas, sung ballads. The oldest is from 1659.”",
    follow: "Which songs do you think will carry our history a hundred years from now?",
    deeper: "In 2023 Maharashtra adopted “Jai Jai Maharashtra Majha”, made famous by Shahir Sable, as its official state song." },

  { id: "mhc-dahi-handi", type: "concept", domain: "anthropology", region: "mumbai", title: "Dahi Handi: the human pyramids", value: 4, cue: "Janmashtami, Govinda teams, or crowd sports",
    emoji: "🪔", wiki: "Dahi Handi",
    fact: "On the day after Janmashtami, teams of Govindas in Mumbai and Thane build human pyramids to break a pot of curd hung high above the street. In 2012 the Jai Jawan team from Jogeshwari built a 9-tier pyramid of 43.79 feet, a Guinness world record. In 2022 Maharashtra declared Dahi Handi an adventure sport.",
    why: "It began as a game about the child Krishna stealing butter. In Mumbai it became a test of teamwork, with prize money running into lakhs.",
    mech: "The heaviest men stand at the base, with lighter ones above and a small child at the top. Each tier must lock arms and hold still while the next climbs. The pots are hung higher every year, so the pyramids grew to nine tiers. Injuries rose too, so the courts stepped in. The Bombay High Court capped the height at 20 feet in 2014, and the Supreme Court agreed in 2016. In 2017 the High Court accepted a new state rule: no height limit, but nobody under 14.",
    models: ["bottlenecks", "goodharts-law"],
    q: "How does Dahi Handi work and why did courts get involved?",
    a: "Govinda teams build human pyramids, heavy men at the base and a child on top, to break a pot of curd. As pots were hung higher, pyramids reached nine tiers and injuries rose, so courts set height and age limits.",
    chain: [["Teams build pyramids to break a pot of curd", ["pyramid", "pot", "curd", "govinda"]], ["Heavy men at the base, a child on top", ["base", "heav", "child", "top", "tier"]], ["Pots hung higher each year, up to 9 tiers and 43.79 feet", ["higher", "nine", "9", "43.79", "record"]], ["Injuries led courts to set height and age limits", ["injur", "court", "20 feet", "14", "age"]]],
    mcq: { q: "What rule did the Bombay High Court accept in 2017 for Dahi Handi?", o: ["No participant under 14 years of age", "A maximum of four tiers", "Only one team per street", "A ban on prize money"] },
    bring: "When Dahi Handi traffic jams come up: “A Jogeshwari team built a nine-tier pyramid, almost 44 feet, in 2012. It is in the Guinness book.”",
    follow: "Should a festival be allowed to carry real risk if people join by choice?",
    deeper: "Big pyramids are built in Spain too. The castells of Catalonia reach ten levels, and the Govindas and castellers have exchanged visits." },

  { id: "mhc-puneri-pagadi", type: "fact", domain: "design", region: "maharashtra", title: "The Puneri pagadi and its GI tag", value: 3, cue: "turbans, Pune pride, or geographical indications",
    emoji: "👳", wiki: "Puneri Pagadi",
    fact: "The Puneri pagadi is the flat, round turban worn by Lokmanya Tilak in almost every photograph. It was introduced in the 19th century by the reformer Mahadev Govind Ranade. In September 2009 it received a Geographical Indication tag, one of the few pieces of clothing in India to get one.",
    why: "A GI tag is usually for food or crafts tied to a place, like Alphonso mangoes. Here a city protected the way it ties a cloth.",
    mech: "A pagadi is a turban made of a long strip of cloth. Earlier Maharashtrian turbans were heavy and wound by hand each day. The Puneri style is pre-made on a frame, light, and the same shape every time, so it suited office-going men of the new city. Tilak, Gokhale and other Pune leaders wore it, and it became a sign of learning and respect. A GI tag means only makers from the Pune area may sell a turban under that name.",
    models: ["moat"],
    q: "What is a Puneri pagadi and why does it have a GI tag?",
    a: "It is the light, pre-made turban introduced by Ranade in the 19th century and made famous by Tilak. In 2009 it received a Geographical Indication tag, so only Pune makers may sell a turban under that name.",
    chain: [["A light, pre-made turban introduced by Ranade", ["ranade", "pre-made", "light", "frame", "19th"]], ["Worn by Tilak and other Pune leaders", ["tilak", "gokhale", "leaders", "photograph", "respect"]], ["Geographical Indication tag in 2009", ["gi", "geographical", "2009", "tag"]], ["Only Pune makers can use the name", ["only", "pune makers", "name", "sell"]]],
    mcq: { q: "What does the GI tag on the Puneri pagadi protect?", o: ["Only Pune-area makers may sell a turban under that name", "The exact colour of the cloth", "The right to wear it in government offices", "Its price"] },
    bring: "When you see Tilak's photo: “That turban has a GI tag, like Alphonso mangoes. Pune registered it in 2009.”",
    follow: "What else from your home town deserves a GI tag?",
    deeper: "The Puneri pagadi is still given as a mark of honour at Pune functions. The Kolhapuri chappal, from the other end of the state, got its GI tag ten years later, in 2019." },

  { id: "mhc-misal-award", type: "fact", domain: "anthropology", region: "mumbai", title: "Misal pav: the world's best vegetarian dish", value: 3, cue: "breakfast, spicy food, or Maharashtrian snacks",
    emoji: "🍲", wiki: "Misal pav",
    fact: "Misal pav is a spicy curry of sprouted moth beans topped with farsan, onion and a fiery red gravy called tarri or kat, eaten with pav. In 2015 the misal at Aaswad, a restaurant in Dadar, Mumbai, was named the world's best vegetarian dish at the Foodie Hub Global Awards in London. Judges looked at about 4,000 dishes from 150 cities.",
    why: "A 30-rupee breakfast from a Dadar canteen beat restaurant dishes from around the world. The dish shows how Maharashtra turns a bean into a feast.",
    mech: "Moth beans, called matki, are sprouted to make them soft and more nourishing. They are cooked into a thin curry called usal. The heat comes from the tarri, a separate red gravy made with Kolhapuri-style chilli and spices, which you add as you like. Farsan on top gives crunch, onion and lime give freshness, and pav soaks up the gravy. Each town has its own version: Kolhapur's is the hottest, Puneri misal is milder, and Nashik's comes with curd.",
    models: [],
    q: "What is misal pav and why was Aaswad's version in the news in 2015?",
    a: "It is a curry of sprouted moth beans with a fiery tarri gravy, topped with farsan and eaten with pav. In 2015 the misal at Aaswad in Dadar won best vegetarian dish at the Foodie Hub Global Awards.",
    chain: [["Sprouted moth beans cooked into usal", ["moth", "matki", "sprout", "usal", "bean"]], ["Fiery tarri gravy, farsan and pav", ["tarri", "kat", "gravy", "farsan", "pav"]], ["Aaswad in Dadar won a world award in 2015", ["aaswad", "dadar", "2015", "foodie hub", "best vegetarian"]], ["Each town has its own version", ["kolhapur", "puneri", "nashik", "version", "town"]]],
    mcq: { q: "Which Mumbai restaurant's misal was named the world's best vegetarian dish in 2015?", o: ["Aaswad, Dadar", "Leopold Café, Colaba", "Kyani, Marine Lines", "Prakash, Dadar"] },
    bring: "When ordering misal: “A Dadar misal was judged the best vegetarian dish in the world in 2015, out of about 4,000 dishes.”",
    follow: "Kolhapuri, Puneri or Nashik misal: which side are you on?",
    deeper: "Misal is often eaten for breakfast. The name comes from the Marathi word for a mixture, since many things are mixed into one plate." },

  { id: "mhc-modak", type: "fact", domain: "anthropology", region: "maharashtra", title: "The modak: Ganpati's steamed sweet", value: 3, cue: "Ganeshotsav, sweets, or Konkan cooking",
    emoji: "🥟", wiki: "Modak",
    fact: "The modak is a sweet dumpling offered to Lord Ganesh, who is called Modakapriya, lover of modaks. The classic ukadiche modak has a thin cover of rice flour, a filling of fresh coconut and jaggery, and is steamed, not fried. Families usually offer 21 modaks on Ganesh Chaturthi.",
    why: "It is the taste of Ganeshotsav for most Maharashtrian families. The shape, pleated like a closed flower bud, takes years of practice.",
    mech: "Rice flour is cooked with hot water into a soft dough, so the cover can be pressed thin without tearing. The filling of grated coconut, jaggery and cardamom is cooked just until it holds together. The cook shapes the dough into a cup, fills it, pinches small pleats and closes the top into a point. The modaks are steamed for about ten minutes and eaten warm with ghee. In the Konkan, rice and coconut are the local staples, which is why the sweet looks the way it does.",
    models: [],
    q: "What is an ukadiche modak and how is it made?",
    a: "A steamed dumpling with a rice-flour cover and a coconut-jaggery filling, offered to Ganesh, who is called Modakapriya. The dough is shaped into a pleated cup, filled, closed to a point and steamed.",
    chain: [["Offered to Ganesh, the Modakapriya", ["ganesh", "ganpati", "modakapriya", "offer", "21"]], ["Rice-flour cover, coconut and jaggery filling", ["rice flour", "rice-flour", "coconut", "jaggery"]], ["Pleated, closed to a point and steamed", ["pleat", "point", "steam", "shape"]], ["Rice and coconut are Konkan staples", ["konkan", "staple", "local"]]],
    mcq: { q: "How is a traditional ukadiche modak cooked?", o: ["Steamed", "Deep fried", "Baked", "Boiled in milk"] },
    bring: "When modaks are served: “Ganesh is called Modakapriya. The steamed ones are Konkan food: rice flour outside, coconut and jaggery inside.”",
    follow: "Which festival sweet in your home takes the most skill to make?",
    deeper: "Fried modaks with a wheat cover keep longer and are common for sending to relatives. Modak-shaped moulds now sell in lakhs before Ganeshotsav." },

  { id: "mhc-sairat", type: "fact", domain: "business", region: "maharashtra", title: "Sairat: a 4-crore Marathi film that earned over 100 crore", value: 4, cue: "Marathi cinema, film budgets, or Nagraj Manjule",
    emoji: "🎞️", wiki: "Sairat",
    fact: "“Sairat” (2016), directed by Nagraj Manjule, was made for about 4 crore rupees and earned around 110 crore. It was the first Marathi film to cross 100 crore and stayed the highest-grossing Marathi film for ten years. Its two leads, Rinku Rajguru and Akash Thosar, had never acted in a film before.",
    why: "It showed that a Marathi film about a small-town love story could compete with Hindi blockbusters in the same cinemas.",
    mech: "The film tells of a college love between a girl from a powerful landowning family and a boy from a poor fisherman's family, and the price they pay. Manjule cast unknown local actors, so the faces felt real. Ajay-Atul recorded the music with a full symphony orchestra in Hollywood, a first for an Indian film, and the songs went viral before release. Word of mouth filled cinemas week after week. The Hindi remake “Dhadak” came in 2018.",
    models: ["network-effects", "social-proof"],
    q: "Why was Sairat such a big success?",
    a: "A 4-crore film with unknown local actors told a small-town love story across caste lines. Hit songs recorded in Hollywood and strong word of mouth took it to around 110 crore, a Marathi record that stood for ten years.",
    chain: [["Made for about 4 crore with unknown actors", ["4 crore", "four crore", "unknown", "never acted", "newcomer", "debut"]], ["A love story across caste and class lines", ["love", "caste", "class", "landowning", "poor"]], ["Hit songs recorded with an orchestra in Hollywood", ["ajay-atul", "hollywood", "orchestra", "songs", "viral"]], ["Word of mouth took it to around 110 crore", ["word of mouth", "110", "100 crore", "record"]]],
    mcq: { q: "Roughly how much did Sairat earn against its 4-crore budget?", o: ["Around 110 crore", "Around 10 crore", "Around 40 crore", "Around 500 crore"] },
    bring: "When film budgets come up: “Sairat cost about 4 crore and made over 100 crore. Both leads had never acted before.”",
    follow: "What makes you recommend a film to friends: the stars, the story or the songs?",
    deeper: "Rinku Rajguru was 14 when filming began and won a special National Award. In 2026 the film “Raja Shivaji” passed Sairat's record for a Marathi film." },

  { id: "mhc-khandoba-jejuri", type: "place", domain: "anthropology", region: "maharashtra", title: "Khandoba of Jejuri and the turmeric festival", value: 4, cue: "family deities, Jejuri, or why a town turns yellow",
    emoji: "🟡", wiki: "Jejuri",
    fact: "Khandoba is the most common family deity, or kuladaivat, in Maharashtra. His main temple is at Jejuri, about 48 km from Pune, on a hill with around 450 steps. Devotees throw turmeric powder, called bhandara, over each other and the temple, so the whole hill turns gold. People call it Sonyachi Jejuri, the golden Jejuri.",
    why: "Khandoba is worshipped by farmers, shepherds, warriors and traders alike, and also by some Muslim families. He is a god who crosses the usual lines.",
    mech: "Khandoba is seen as a form of Shiva who rode out as a warrior to kill the demons Mani and Malla. Turmeric is linked to the sun and to blessings, so it became his offering. Over 600 Khandoba temples exist across the Deccan, and families return to Jejuri for weddings and vows. The biggest festival is Champa Shashthi in the month of Margashirsha, usually December. Muslim devotees have long called him Mallu Khan, and by tradition a Muslim family looks after the temple horses.",
    models: [],
    q: "Who is Khandoba and why does Jejuri turn yellow?",
    a: "Khandoba is Maharashtra's most common family deity, a warrior form of Shiva, with his main temple on a hill at Jejuri near Pune. Devotees throw turmeric, bhandara, over everything, so the hill turns gold, especially at Champa Shashthi in December.",
    chain: [["The most common family deity in Maharashtra", ["family deity", "kuladaivat", "kuldaivat", "most common", "most popular"]], ["A warrior form of Shiva who killed Mani and Malla", ["shiva", "warrior", "mani", "malla", "demon"]], ["Turmeric, bhandara, is thrown over everything", ["turmeric", "bhandara", "yellow", "gold"]], ["Champa Shashthi festival in December", ["champa", "shashthi", "margashirsha", "december"]]],
    mcq: { q: "Why is Jejuri called “Sonyachi Jejuri”, the golden Jejuri?", o: ["Devotees cover it in yellow turmeric powder", "The temple roof is made of gold", "The sun sets directly behind the temple", "Gold was once mined on the hill"] },
    bring: "When someone mentions Jejuri: “Devotees throw so much turmeric that the whole hill turns gold. That is why they call it Sonyachi Jejuri.”",
    follow: "Do you know which deity your family returns to for weddings and vows?",
    deeper: "The chant at Jejuri is “Yelkot Yelkot Jai Malhar”. Khandoba is also worshipped in Karnataka as Mailara and in Andhra as Mallanna." },

  { id: "mhc-samyukta-maharashtra", type: "event", domain: "history", region: "mumbai", title: "The Samyukta Maharashtra movement and the 106 martyrs", value: 5, cue: "Maharashtra Day, Hutatma Chowk, or why Mumbai is the capital",
    emoji: "🔥", wiki: "Hutatma Chowk",
    fact: "In 1955 a central commission proposed that Bombay city should not go to a Marathi state. People across Maharashtra rose in protest. On 21 November 1955 police fired on a crowd at Flora Fountain, and in all 106 people died during the movement. On 1 May 1960 the state of Maharashtra was formed with Mumbai as its capital.",
    why: "Maharashtra Day marks this, and Flora Fountain was renamed Hutatma Chowk, the martyrs' square, in their memory. Mumbai's place as the capital was won, not given.",
    mech: "After Independence, states were being redrawn by language. Bombay city had a Marathi majority but most of its big business was Gujarati-owned, so the plan was to make it a bilingual state or a separate city-state. Marathi people saw this as losing their main city. Writers like Acharya Atre, through his newspaper Maratha, and shahirs like Amar Shaikh and Annabhau Sathe kept the movement alive for five years. In 1960 Bombay State was split into Maharashtra and Gujarat.",
    models: ["incentives", "commitment-consistency"],
    q: "What was the Samyukta Maharashtra movement?",
    a: "A five-year movement from 1955 for a Marathi state with Bombay as its capital, after a commission proposed keeping the city out. Police firing at Flora Fountain and elsewhere killed 106 people. Maharashtra was formed on 1 May 1960.",
    chain: [["States were being redrawn by language", ["language", "linguistic", "redrawn", "commission", "1955"]], ["The plan kept Bombay out of a Marathi state", ["bombay", "out", "bilingual", "city-state", "capital"]], ["Police firing at Flora Fountain; 106 martyrs", ["flora fountain", "firing", "fired", "106", "martyr", "hutatma"]], ["Maharashtra formed on 1 May 1960", ["1 may", "1960", "formed", "gujarat"]]],
    mcq: { q: "What does Hutatma Chowk in south Mumbai commemorate?", o: ["The 106 people who died in the Samyukta Maharashtra movement", "Soldiers of the 1971 war", "Victims of the 1944 docks explosion", "Freedom fighters of 1857"] },
    bring: "At Flora Fountain: “This is Hutatma Chowk. 106 people died so that Mumbai would be the capital of Maharashtra.”",
    follow: "Do you think states should still be drawn by language today?",
    deeper: "The Hutatma Chowk memorial shows two figures holding a torch. The movement's leaders came from across parties: S. M. Joshi, S. A. Dange, Acharya Atre, Senapati Bapat and Prabodhankar Thackeray." }

